 "what_i_did": "Read lab/TASKS.md, tasks/longhorizon/spec.md, dev.json and the checker code in lab/core.py; wrote 21 distinct multi-mode tasks (4-6 turns) on categorical state: hand-offs, holder swaps, open/closed and on/off flips, object locations, bulk moves, swaps of container contents, device settings, corrections, conditional recolouring, role rotation, room bookings. Answers are a name/colour on an ANSWER line (exact_any), a labelled echo line (anchored regex) or a non-strict JSON object of 2-3 slots (keys + equals). No sums, no ordering questions, no tool mode.",
 "verification": "Each task is defined in a Python generator as an initial state plus one state-update function per turn; the expected answer is produced by simulating the updates, not typed by hand. The generator asserts for every task: (a) dropping any single state-changing update changes the final answer (three overwritten-by-design turns are exempt: g2-01 turn 2, which is instead checked by a reply_check on reply 2, and the turns later replaced by an explicit correction in g2-06 and g2-11); (b) the final answer differs from the start state and from every intermediate state; (c) the negative is the answer obtained by dropping exactly one named update (tags.negative_drops_turn) and differs from the reference; (d) turns stay under 900 characters. Final states of the medium and hard tasks were also re-traced by hand against the turn text. The model was NOT run on these tasks.",
 "caveats": [
  "Difficulty labels are estimates from the spec's baseline observations, not measured (held-out, no pilot).",
  "JSON slot values must be the bare word (e.g. 'blue', not 'blue bowl'); prompts state the allowed values, but a verbose correct answer would be scored wrong.",
  "g2-01, g2-06, g2-11 each contain one update that is overwritten later by design, so losing that one update alone does not change the final answer (g2-01 covers it with a reply_check)."
